["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"BOLA2","referenceIdentifier":"Q9H3K6","compartmentNames":["cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q9H3K6","disease":false},{"dbId":"8862589","stId":"R-HSA-8862589","id":"R-HSA-8862589","name":"LMO7","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"LMO7","referenceIdentifier":"Q8WWI1","compartmentNames":["cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q8WWI1","disease":false},{"dbId":"217207","stId":"R-GGA-217207","id":"R-GGA-217207","name":"FANCJ (BRIP1), Fanconi anemia group J protein homolog","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Gallus gallus"],"summation":"BRIP1 (also called BACH1) is a DEAH helicase that interacts with BRCT domain of BRCA1. It has an important role in BRCA1-dependent DNA reapir and checkpoint functions. However, the brip1 mutant DT40 cells have phenotype that is more closely resemble fancc mutants with profound sensitivity to DNA-alkylating agents cisplatin and acute cell-cycle arrest in late S-G2 phase. Thus, BRIP1 has a function in the Fanconi anemia <span class=\"highlighting\" >pathway</span> that is independent of BRCA1 and downstream of FANCD2 activation.","referenceName":"BRIP1","referenceIdentifier":"Q3YK19","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q3YK19","disease":false},{"dbId":"8862709","stId":"R-HSA-8862709","id":"R-HSA-8862709","name":"CMKLR1","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Chemokine-like receptor 1 (CMKLR1, ERV1, CHEMR23, DEZ) is activated by the essentil fatty acid-derived, pro-inflammation resolving ligand resolvin E1 (RvE1), which is the result of sequential enzymatic conversion of the omega-3 fatty acid eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) by aspirin-modified cyclooxygenase or cytochrome P450 and 5-lipoxygenase. RvE1 is produced in both 18(S)- and 18(R)-stereoisomeric forms (Arita et al. 2005, 2007).<br><br>CMKLR1 is also reported to be a receptor for chemerin (Wittamer et al. 2003) and RvE1 is reported to be a partial agonist for Leukotriene B4 receptor 1 (LTB4R, BLT1, CMKRL1)  (Arita et al. 2007).<br><br>CMKLR1 signals via the Akt/rS6/mTOR <span class=\"highlighting\" >pathway</span> (Ohira et al. 2010). This RvE1 mediated signaling influences is believed to actively promote the resolution of inflammation (Freire et al. 2017).","referenceName":"CMKLR1","referenceIdentifier":"Q99788","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q99788","disease":false},{"dbId":"9931643","stId":"R-HSA-9931643","id":"R-HSA-9931643","name":"GalNAc-S202, T203-TFPIβ","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceIsoform","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"TFPI","referenceIdentifier":"P10646","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/P10646-2","disease":false},{"dbId":"4839621","stId":"R-HSA-4839621","id":"R-HSA-4839621","name":"pT41,S45-CTNNB1 S37F","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"CTNNB1","referenceIdentifier":"P35222","compartmentNames":["cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005829"],"isDisease":true,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/P35222","disease":true},{"dbId":"6802523","stId":"R-HSA-6802523","id":"R-HSA-6802523","name":"ESRP1(1-606)-p-RAF1(227-648) fu